# Cloudflare Pages response headers for the whole published site.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The machine artifacts (`/docs/llms*.txt` and every
# `.md` twin of a docs page) are only useful if they arrive as TEXT. Every one
# of the design systems this repo was benchmarked against — spectrum.adobe.com,
# polaris.shopify.com, primer.style — serves an llms.txt endpoint that returns
# 200 with `text/html`, because the request fell through to a single-page-app
# shell and the agent read a page of markup instead of the documentation.
#
# Cloudflare Pages does map `.txt` and `.md` to text/plain by default. Declaring
# it makes that a property of this deployment rather than of a default that a
# future config, framework adapter or redirect rule could change silently.
# `scripts/check-llms-content-type.mjs <base-url>` asserts it against a running
# deployment.
#
# WHY THERE ARE NO PER-PROJECT RULES. An earlier version of this file also
# listed `/docs/*/llms.txt`, `/docs/*/llms-full.txt` and so on, to spell out the
# scoped design systems (`/docs/southleft/...`). Those rules were redundant and
# actively harmful: in Cloudflare `_headers`, `*` matches ACROSS `/`, so
# `/docs/*.txt` already covers `/docs/southleft/llms.txt`. Both rules matched,
# Cloudflare applied both, and the response carried the value twice --
# `content-type: text/plain; charset=utf-8, text/plain; charset=utf-8` --
# observed live on every southleft artifact while the unscoped `/docs/llms.txt`
# was clean. That is a malformed field (RFC 9110 s8.3 allows exactly one
# Content-Type, and the joined value is not a valid media type), so a strict
# agent HTTP client may reject or mis-sniff it -- precisely the "does it arrive
# as text" failure this file exists to prevent. One rule per extension is both
# sufficient and correct; check-llms-content-type.mjs now fails on a
# comma-joined content-type so the class cannot come back silently.
#
# HOW THIS REACHES THE ROOT. `pnpm run copy:pages-root` copies pages-root/ into
# dist/ during build:all, so this lands at dist/_headers. Cloudflare reads
# _headers ONLY from the published root -- a nested copy is ignored.
#
# It used to ride along inside apps/home/public/, because apps/home was the one
# app that built straight into dist/. The homepage was dropped from the deploy
# on 2026-08-27, which would have silently stopped shipping this file: the
# artifacts would have kept serving, just with the wrong content-type and no
# error anywhere. Hence pages-root/ -- see its README.

/docs/*.txt
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff

/docs/*.md
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff

/docs/*/status.json
  Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8
